<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Sapienza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1209">1209</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Sapienza was an agreement of 1209 between the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-i-of-villehardouin">Geoffrey I of Villehardouin</a>, ruler of the Frankish Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Named after the island of Sapienza off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a>, it recognised Venetian possession of the ports of Modon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>, while Geoffrey acknowledged Venetian overlordship for his principality and granted Venetians trading privileges.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204, the Partitio Romaniae assigned most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, or Morea, to the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthia">Corinthia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argolid">Argolis</a>. Venice did not enforce its claim at once, and in 1205 a few hundred crusaders under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-of-champlitte">William of Champlitte</a> and Geoffrey of Villehardouin conquered much of the peninsula on their own account, breaking the main Greek resistance at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-olive-grove-of-koundouros">Battle of the Olive Grove of Koundouros</a>. A Venetian fleet took the Messenian peninsula with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a> from Champlitte's men in 1206 or 1207.</p><p>Late in 1208 Champlitte left to take up his inheritance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and Villehardouin succeeded him, using feudal law to keep Champlitte's nephew from the title. Negotiations probably began at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ravennika">Parliament of Ravennika</a> in May 1209, likely through the mediation of the Latin Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-flanders">Henry of Flanders</a>, and the treaty was signed in June.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> recognised Geoffrey as master of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> as far as Corinth, except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methoni">Modon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koroni">Coron</a>. Geoffrey became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Republic in a perpetual alliance; he and his heirs were to become Venetian citizens, keep a house in Venice and send each year two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> brocades for St Mark's Basilica and one for the Doge. Venetian merchants were freed from all tariffs and could have a church, a market and a court in every city of the principality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> gained a valuable outpost and protected its trade, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> won the support of the Venetian fleet for a largely theoretical overlordship, and Geoffrey secured Venice's backing against the Champlitte claimants; by 1210 the pope recognised him as princeps Achaiae. The text is also a unique source for the young principality. The French medievalist Antoine Bon noted that the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea">Chronicle of the Morea</a> presents its feudal order as complete by 1209 or 1210, whereas the treaty shows it still taking shape. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a> was then still held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> held out until 1211/12, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monemvasia">Monemvasia</a> surrendered only in 1248. The treaty also preserves the names of many lords not otherwise recorded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Sapienza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Venetian forces occupied Modon (Methoni) and Coron (Koroni) around 1207, and the <b>Treaty of Sapienza</b> with Geoffrey I of Villehardouin of the Principality of Achaea confirmed them in 1209.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-modon-and-coron">Venetian Modon and Coron</a></li></ul></section>
